With Inertia SSR off, `<Head>` only mutates the document head after JavaScript hydrates. It will not appear in the initial Laravel-rendered HTML source, so crawlers that rely on raw HTML may miss `noindex`.

For crawler-critical directives, put them server-side:

```blade
{{-- resources/views/app.blade.php --}}
@if($page['props']['noindex'] ?? false)
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">
@endif
```

Or better for hard SEO directives, send an HTTP header from the route/controller:

```php
return Inertia::render('SomePage')
    ->toResponse($request)
    ->withHeaders([
        'X-Robots-Tag' => 'noindex, nofollow',
    ]);
```

Use Inertia `<Head>` for client-visible titles/meta where JS rendering is acceptable. Use Blade or `X-Robots-Tag` for anything crawlers must see without JS. If you need dynamic per-page SEO in the initial HTML broadly, turn Inertia SSR back on.

With Inertia SSR turned off, `<Head>` is client-side only. It mutates the DOM after JavaScript boots, so it will not appear in the initial HTML “view source” response. Crawlers that do not execute JS, or that make indexing decisions before rendering JS, may miss your `noindex`.

For `noindex`, handle it server-side in Laravel, because it is a crawler directive and should be present in the first response.

Good options:

```blade
{{-- resources/views/app.blade.php --}}
@if (! empty($page['props']['meta']['noindex']))
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">
@endif
```

Then pass it from your controller/Inertia response:

```php
return Inertia::render('SomePage', [
    'meta' => [
        'noindex' => true,
    ],
]);
```

Even stronger for non-HTML/PDF-like responses or pages where you want a guaranteed directive:

```php
return Inertia::render('SomePage', [...])
    ->toResponse($request)
    ->headers->set('X-Robots-Tag', 'noindex, nofollow');
```

In short: keep `<Head>` for browser-visible/title/social metadata if you want, but do not rely on it for critical SEO directives without SSR. Either enable Inertia SSR, render the robots meta tag in the root Blade template, or send `X-Robots-Tag` from Laravel.

With Inertia SSR turned off, anything you put in Inertia’s `<Head>` is injected client-side after the initial HTML is served. So it may appear in DevTools, but it will not be present in “View Page Source” or the raw HTML response many crawlers first see.

For `noindex`, do not rely on client-side `<Head>`.

Handle it server-side instead:

```php
// routes/web.php
Route::get('/private-page', function () {
    return Inertia::render('PrivatePage')
        ->withViewData([
            'robots' => 'noindex, nofollow',
        ]);
});
```

Then in your root Blade template, usually `resources/views/app.blade.php`:

```blade
@if (!empty($robots))
    <meta name="robots" content="{{ $robots }}">
@endif
```

Even better for `noindex`, send an HTTP header where appropriate:

```php
return Inertia::render('PrivatePage')
    ->toResponse($request)
    ->header('X-Robots-Tag', 'noindex, nofollow');
```

Recommended approach:

Use Inertia `<Head>` for normal client-side title/meta updates, but put crawler-critical directives like `noindex`, canonical tags, and important SEO defaults in the initial server-rendered Blade response or HTTP headers. If you want all Inertia head tags visible in raw HTML, enable Inertia SSR.

Use Stripe’s async webhook verifier, not the sync `constructEvent`.

In Workers, the sync path can hit Node crypto APIs. The Workers-compatible pattern is:

```ts
import Stripe from 'stripe'
import { Hono } from 'hono'

type Bindings = {
  STRIPE_SECRET_KEY: string
  STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET: string
}

const app = new Hono<{ Bindings: Bindings }>()

app.post('/stripe/webhook', async (c) => {
  const signature = c.req.header('stripe-signature')
  if (!signature) return c.text('Missing signature', 400)

  const stripe = new Stripe(c.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY)

  try {
    // Important: read raw body before any JSON parsing
    const body = await c.req.text()

    const event = await stripe.webhooks.constructEventAsync(
      body,
      signature,
      c.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET
    )

    switch (event.type) {
      case 'checkout.session.completed':
        // handle event.data.object
        break
    }

    return c.text('ok', 200)
  } catch (err) {
    return c.text('Invalid signature', 400)
  }
})

export default app
```

The two rules are:

1. Use `stripe.webhooks.constructEventAsync(...)`.
2. Pass the exact raw request body from `await c.req.text()`. Do not call `json()`, use Hono validators, or otherwise parse the body before verification.

Hono’s own Stripe webhook example now shows this exact `context.req.text()` plus `constructEventAsync` pattern, and Cloudflare’s Stripe Workers guidance points at Web Crypto / async verification for Workers runtimes. Sources: Hono example lines 100-126 and Cloudflare’s Workers Stripe example lines 120-145.  
https://hono.dev/examples/stripe-webhook  
https://blog.cloudflare.com/announcing-stripe-support-in-workers/

Use Stripe’s async webhook verifier, not the sync Node path.

In Workers, the fix is:

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono'
import Stripe from 'stripe'

type Env = {
  STRIPE_SECRET_KEY: string
  STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET: string
}

const app = new Hono<{ Bindings: Env }>()

app.post('/stripe/webhook', async (c) => {
  const stripe = new Stripe(c.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY)
  const signature = c.req.header('stripe-signature')

  if (!signature) return c.text('Missing Stripe signature', 400)

  try {
    const rawBody = await c.req.text()

    const event = await stripe.webhooks.constructEventAsync(
      rawBody,
      signature,
      c.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET
    )

    switch (event.type) {
      case 'checkout.session.completed':
        // handle event.data.object
        break
    }

    return c.text('ok', 200)
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Stripe webhook verification failed:', err)
    return c.text('Invalid signature', 400)
  }
})

export default app
```

The important bits:

- Use `constructEventAsync`, not `constructEvent`.
- Read the body with `await c.req.text()` before any JSON parsing.
- Do not call `await c.req.json()` first; Stripe verification needs the exact raw body.
- Make sure the secret is the webhook signing secret, `whsec_...`, not your Stripe API key.
- Recent Hono docs show this exact Workers-compatible pattern: raw body via `context.req.text()` plus `stripe.webhooks.constructEventAsync()`.

Older examples sometimes pass `Stripe.createSubtleCryptoProvider()` as the 5th argument. That was the common workaround for Web Crypto in Workers. With current Stripe SDK/Hono examples, `constructEventAsync(...)` is usually enough, but if your installed Stripe version complains about crypto provider, upgrade `stripe` first.

