This is very likely your custom external store doing a render-phase notification, and React 19 is making it louder/more visible.

Things to audit first:

1. `getSnapshot` must be pure
   It should only read store state. It must not initialize state, normalize data by writing back, call listeners, call `setState`, dispatch, or mutate the store.

2. `getSnapshot` must return a stable cached value
   If it returns `{ ...state }`, `array.map(...)`, a new selector object, etc. on every call, React may see the store as changed during render. React’s docs explicitly require caching snapshots unless the underlying store changed: https://react.dev/reference/react/useSyncExternalStore

3. `subscribe` must not synchronously call the callback during subscription
   This is a common bug:

```ts
function subscribe(callback) {
  listeners.add(callback)
  callback() // bad: can update during render/subscribe timing
  return () => listeners.delete(callback)
}
```

Use:

```ts
function subscribe(callback) {
  listeners.add(callback)
  return () => listeners.delete(callback)
}
```

4. Store writes should notify after mutation, outside render
   Avoid patterns where a component render calls a store setter, selector registration mutates state, or lazy store hydration happens inside `getSnapshot`.

A healthy shape is:

```ts
let state = initialState
let snapshot = state
const listeners = new Set<() => void>()

export function getSnapshot() {
  return snapshot
}

export function setStore(next) {
  state = typeof next === 'function' ? next(state) : next
  snapshot = state
  listeners.forEach((listener) => listener())
}

export function subscribe(listener) {
  listeners.add(listener)
  return () => listeners.delete(listener)
}
```

If your store is mutable, keep a version number and cached immutable snapshot; only rebuild the snapshot when the version changes. Also check whether any selector wrapper returns a fresh object each render.

