1. For most Postgres/libpq clients the parameter is `connect_timeout`, not `connection_timeout`.
2. Even with the right parameter, it only covers establishing the TCP/Postgres connection. Your failure may be the first SQL statement waiting on Neon compute/storage readiness, so your app/query timeout can still fire.

The fix is to treat branch creation as eventually ready. After creating the branch, run a warmup/readiness loop before migrations, tests, or preview boot:

```ts
for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
  try {
    await db.query("select 1");
    break;
  } catch (err) {
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Math.min(1000 * 2 ** i, 10000)));
  }
}
```

