import { toNextJsHandler } from "better-auth/next-js";

export const { GET, POST } = toNextJsHandler(auth);
```

Since you’re using `pnpm`, I’d also check for duplicate/mismatched Better Auth versions:

```bash
pnpm why better-auth
pnpm update better-auth
```

The most suspicious line is any server-side call like:

```ts
auth.api.acceptInvitation({ body: { invitationId } })
```

without `headers: await headers()`. That can produce exactly this kind of “user is logged in, but session is undefined inside Better Auth” failure.

This is usually caused by calling the server API without forwarding the request headers/cookies.

For Better Auth’s organization plugin, `acceptInvitation` requires session cookies. In a Next.js App Router server action or route handler, do this:

```ts
