With Inertia SSR turned off, `<Head>` is only applied after the JavaScript app hydrates. So `view-source:` and crawlers that don’t execute JS will not see your `noindex` tag, even though it appears in the live DOM client-side.

For SEO-critical tags like `noindex`, render them from Laravel in the initial Blade response instead of relying on Inertia’s client-side `<Head>`.

Example:

```blade
{{-- resources/views/app.blade.php --}}
<head>
    @if(!empty($page['props']['meta']['noindex']))
        <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">
    @endif

    @inertiaHead
</head>
```

And pass it from Laravel:

```php
return Inertia::render('SomePage', [
    'meta' => [
        'noindex' => true,
    ],
]);
```

Or set it directly in the controller / middleware and share it globally.

Best rule of thumb: use Inertia `<Head>` for client-side title/meta updates, but use server-rendered Blade meta tags or enable Inertia SSR for anything crawlers must see in the initial HTML. For `noindex`, I’d treat server-rendering as mandatory.

With Inertia SSR turned off, `<Head>` only updates the DOM after JavaScript hydrates. It will not appear in the initial HTML source, so crawlers that depend on raw HTML may miss your `noindex`.

Put crawler-critical directives somewhere Laravel renders server-side:

```blade
{{-- resources/views/app.blade.php --}}
@if($noindex ?? false)
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex,nofollow">
@endif
```

Set `$noindex` from a controller, middleware, route metadata, or shared layout data before returning the Inertia response.

For even stronger handling, use an HTTP header:

```php
return Inertia::render('Page')
    ->toResponse($request)
    ->headers->set('X-Robots-Tag', 'noindex, nofollow');
```

Use `<Head>` for client-visible titles/meta, but use Blade or `X-Robots-Tag` for SEO directives that crawlers must see without JS. Verify with `curl` or “View Page Source”, not DevTools Elements, since DevTools shows the hydrated DOM.

Use Stripe’s async Web Crypto path, not the sync Node verifier.

In Workers/Hono, the usual pattern is:

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono'
import Stripe from 'stripe'

type Env = {
  STRIPE_SECRET_KEY: string
  STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET: string
}

const app = new Hono<{ Bindings: Env }>()

app.post('/stripe/webhook', async (c) => {
  const stripe = new Stripe(c.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, {
    httpClient: Stripe.createFetchHttpClient(),
  })

  const signature = c.req.header('stripe-signature')
  if (!signature) return c.text('Missing stripe-signature', 400)

  const body = await c.req.text() // raw body, read once

  try {
    const event = await stripe.webhooks.constructEventAsync(
      body,
      signature,
      c.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET,
      undefined,
      Stripe.createSubtleCryptoProvider()
    )

    switch (event.type) {
      case 'checkout.session.completed':
        // handle event.data.object
        break
    }

    return c.text('ok', 200)
  } catch (err) {
    return c.text('Invalid signature', 400)
  }
})

export default app
```

Key bits:

- Use `constructEventAsync`, not `constructEvent`.
- Pass `Stripe.createSubtleCryptoProvider()` so verification uses Web Crypto.
- Use `await c.req.text()` before any JSON/body parser touches the request.
- Do not call `await c.req.json()` first; Stripe verification needs the exact raw payload.
- `Stripe.createFetchHttpClient()` is the Workers-friendly HTTP client.

