This is usually caused by accepting the invite from a server call that does **not pass the incoming request headers/cookies** into Better Auth. The user can be logged in in the browser, but `auth.api.acceptInvitation(...)` sees no session, so the organization plugin hits `undefined.session`.

In a Next.js 15 App Router server action/route handler, do this:

```ts
"use server";

import { auth } from "@/lib/auth";
import { headers } from "next/headers";

export async function acceptInvite(invitationId: string) {
  return auth.api.acceptInvitation({
    body: { invitationId },
    headers: await headers(),
  });
}
```

Also check these:

```ts
// app/api/auth/[...all]/route.ts
import { auth } from "@/lib/auth";
import { toNextJsHandler } from "better-auth/next-js";

export const { GET, POST } = toNextJsHandler(auth);
```

```ts
// lib/auth-client.ts
import { createAuthClient } from "better-auth/react";
import { organizationClient } from "better-auth/client/plugins";

export const authClient = createAuthClient({
  plugins: [organizationClient()],
});
```

And with pnpm, make sure you do not have duplicated/mismatched Better Auth packages:

```bash
pnpm why better-auth
pnpm dedupe
```

So the short fix is: pass `headers: await headers()` to the server-side invitation accept call, and ensure the auth route/client/server plugin all share the same Better Auth instance/base path. Better Auth’s docs show server API calls requiring `headers: await headers()` for session-backed endpoints, and the organization docs show the same pattern for organization endpoints.

Most likely: you’re calling the **server API** `auth.api.acceptInvitation()` without forwarding request headers/cookies.

In Next.js App Router, the Better Auth docs show:

```ts
import { headers } from "next/headers";

await auth.api.acceptInvitation({
  body: {
    invitationId,
  },
  headers: await headers(),
});
```

That endpoint requires session cookies. If `headers` are missing, Better Auth can’t hydrate the session even though the browser user is logged in, and the org plugin can blow up around `session` instead of giving a clean unauthenticated error.

If you’re accepting from a client component, prefer the client plugin call:

```ts
await authClient.organization.acceptInvitation({
  invitationId,
});
```

Also check these two common pnpm/Better Auth gotchas:

```bash
pnpm why better-auth
pnpm why @better-auth/*
```

Make sure you don’t have mismatched Better Auth package versions. Better Auth’s June 2026 security note recommends updating all directly installed Better Auth packages, not only `better-auth`, and currently points stable projects at latest `1.6.x`.

Useful refs:

- Better Auth org invitation docs: https://better-auth.com/docs/plugins/organization
- Better Auth June 2026 update/version guidance: https://better-auth.com/blog/security-update-june-2026

That error usually means the server-side `acceptInvitation` call is being made without request headers, so Better Auth can’t read the session cookie. The user can be logged in in the browser, but `auth.api.acceptInvitation()` will still see no session unless you forward `headers()`.

In a Next.js 15 app router server action / route handler, do:

```ts
import { headers } from "next/headers";
import { auth } from "@/lib/auth";

export async function acceptInvite(invitationId: string) {
  const data = await auth.api.acceptInvitation({
    body: { invitationId },
    headers: await headers(),
  });

  return data;
}
```

If you’re accepting from a client component, the simpler path is:

```ts
await authClient.organization.acceptInvitation({
  invitationId,
});
```

Also verify these common Better Auth org-plugin setup bits:

```ts
// server auth config
import { organization } from "better-auth/plugins";

export const auth = betterAuth({
  plugins: [organization()],
