Example warmup logic:

```ts
import { Client } from "pg";

export async function waitForNeon(databaseUrl: string) {
  const deadline = Date.now() + 60_000;

  while (Date.now() < deadline) {
    const client = new Client({
      connectionString: databaseUrl,
      connectionTimeoutMillis: 10_000,
      query_timeout: 10_000,
    });

    try {
      await client.connect();
      await client.query("select 1");
      await client.end();
      return;
    } catch (err) {
      try {
        await client.end();
      } catch {}

      await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 2_000));
    }
  }

  throw new Error("Neon branch did not become ready within 60s");
}
```

If you use Prisma, also check whether preview setup is accidentally using the pooled PgBouncer URL for migrations/introspection. Runtime can use pooled; migrations/schema work should use the direct URL via `directUrl`.

Neon’s own docs recommend handling this with longer client-level connection timeouts, retry logic, same-region placement, or disabling/extending scale-to-zero on paid plans. For branch previews, I’d do the warmup poll regardless. It makes the deployment deterministic instead of making the first real user request discover database readiness.

