In general, for a React component that uses a WebSocket plus an interval, cleanup should usually do **both**:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const socket = new WebSocket(url)

  const intervalId = window.setInterval(() => {
    // heartbeat / polling / reconnect check
  }, 1000)

  socket.onmessage = (event) => {
    // handle message
  }

  return () => {
    clearInterval(intervalId)

    socket.onopen = null
    socket.onmessage = null
    socket.onerror = null
    socket.onclose = null

    if (
      socket.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN ||
      socket.readyState === WebSocket.CONNECTING
    ) {
      socket.close()
    }
  }
}, [url])
```

Clearing the interval is good, but it is **not sufficient by itself** if the WebSocket remains open or still has active event handlers. To avoid leaks, the cleanup should also close the WebSocket and detach listeners/handlers.

